WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.999
Let's get into how you actually author a skill.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.000
A skill is really just a folder with a skill.md file inside of it, and Claude finds it on its own.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
For the next few minutes, we'll walk through where skills live, what the front matter holds,

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.000
and why the description is the one part you have to get right. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
A skill has less structure than people expect.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
It's a folder, a markdown file inside it, and that's genuinely the whole requirement.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
The skill directory is a named folder holding a skill.md file.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
That folder name is the skill's name, and it's how you invoke it.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.000
It gets discovered automatically, with no registration step anywhere.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.000
The skill.md body is the markdown that sits below the front matter.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:55.000
It's the actual set of instructions Claude follows, and it always loads, even when there's no front matter at all.

12
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
The front matter itself is an optional YAML block at the very top.

13
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Every field in it is optional.

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
The one field genuinely worth adding is the description,

15
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
because that's what Claude reads to decide whether the skill fits what you asked for.

16
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Directory, body, front matter.

17
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.000
The first thing to internalize is that a skill is a directory, not a loose file.

18
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The layout is a .claude folder, a skills folder inside it,

19
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
and then a named folder for your skill that holds the skill.md.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
That folder name matters more than you'd expect.

21
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.000
It becomes the skill's name and the handle you use to invoke it.

22
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Claude discovers every skill folder that's present automatically.

23
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.689
There's no registry to update, no manifest to edit, and nothing to declare somewhere else to make

24
00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:46.931
the skill visible.

25
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Drop the folder in the right place, and it's found.

26
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
When you want to run a specific skill directly, you call it by that folder name.

27
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
The directory name really is the entire addressing scheme here.

28
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Here's the part that trips almost everyone up, so slow down on it.

29
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.000
The front matter is optional.

30
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
A skill.md file with no front matter block at all is still a perfectly working skill.

31
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
The markdown body loads, and Claude runs it.

32
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Invoking the skill by its folder name still works with zero fields declared.

33
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
There is no silent failure lurking behind a missing front matter block.

34
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
That idea that leaving out front matter quietly breaks the skill is simply wrong.

35
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
The body is the substance of the skill, and it loads either way.

36
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.600
Front matter is there to tune how the skill behaves, which model it uses, which tools it can touch,

37
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.933
when it should fire.

38
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.000
But the skill exists and runs the moment the body exists.

39
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
So, what can the front matter actually hold?

40
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Quite a lot, and all of it optional.

41
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Name sets the display label, and description tells Claude when the skill fits a request.

42
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
A when to use field gives a plain language cue about the situations it should handle,

43
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
and an argument hint field signals the arguments the skill expects when you invoke it directly.

44
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Allowed tools and disallowed tools aren't symmetric.

45
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.727
Allowed tools pre-approves the tools you list, so they skip the permission prompt while every other

46
00:03:17.727 --> 00:03:18.942
tool stays available.

47
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Disallowed tools is the one that actually removes tools from the pool.

48
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
A paths field restricts the skill to certain file locations.

49
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Model and effort let you pick a model and set reasoning effort.

50
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.158
Set context to fork, and the skill runs in an isolated sub-agent, paired with the agent field, to

51
00:03:38.158 --> 00:03:39.936
choose which one handles it.

52
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.000
A hooks field wires in lifecycle behavior.

53
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Of all these fields, the description is the one that carries real weight, so it's worth writing carefully.

54
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:56.000
Claude reads the description to decide whether a given skill fits the request in front of it.

55
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.000
That's the whole matching mechanism.

56
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
If the description is vague, the skill gets passed over in situations where it should have fired.

57
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:10.000
If it's sharp and specific, auto-invocation becomes reliable, and the skill fires exactly when it should.

58
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Everything else in the front matter tunes how the skill behaves once it's chosen.

59
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000
The description is what gets it chosen in the first place.

60
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
So spend your effort there.

61
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
Say plainly what the skill does and the kinds of requests it's meant to handle.

62
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Now for a quick myth-clearing pass, because a few fields get quoted that simply don't exist.

63
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.000
There is no trigger field.

64
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Matching runs off the description, not off a separate trigger declaration.

65
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.000
If you've seen a skill written with a trigger field, that field does nothing.

66
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:46.000
There is no version field baked into the format, and there is no author tags field either.

67
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.426
And to say it one more time, because it's the stickiest myth, a missing front matter block doesn't

68
00:04:52.426 --> 00:04:53.934
cause a silent failure.

69
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.000
The body loads and the skill runs.

70
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Reach for the real fields, the description, the tool scoping, the paths.

71
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Invented fields just sit there inert and mislead the next person who opens the file expecting them to matter.

72
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.000
Let's tie invocation together, because there are two clean ways it happens.

73
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.000
The default is auto-invocation.

74
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.971
Claude reads each skill's description, matches it against what you asked for, and runs the fitting

75
00:05:20.971 --> 00:05:22.949
skill on its own without you naming it.

76
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.000
This is why the description does so much work.

77
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.000
The other way is direct invocation.

78
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:35.000
You call the skill yourself by its folder name, which is handy when you already know exactly which skill you want.

79
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.000
Same skill, same folder, two paths to reach it.

80
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Auto-invocation leans entirely on a good description.

81
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Direct invocation leans on you knowing the folder name.

82
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Between them, a well-named folder and a well-written description cover almost everything you'll want to do.

83
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.242
Authoring a skill comes down to the folder, the markdown file, and a description sharp enough that

84
00:05:59.242 --> 00:06:02.936
Claude recognizes when the work in front of it is a match.

85
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:10.000
That description is the whole matching service, so a vague one leaves good instructions sitting unused.

86
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.444
Keep in mind that the description is everything when it comes to tool matching, so you have to get

87
00:06:15.444 --> 00:06:18.944
it right and it has to be a good match for the actual workload.

88
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.000
And that's where we'll leave this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

